\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The data layer. Two sources are in use --- DENTEX (the only panoramic dataset pairing tooth position with all four reward-relevant pathology classes) and the Tufts Dental Database (per-tooth boxes plus an expert pathology layer whose periapical class maps onto the DENTEX taxonomy). Eight further public panoramic datasets are catalogued as candidates and deliberately \emph  {not} used: each would be promoted only after its annotation semantics are verified end-to-end, and two loaders currently stop at exactly one open question rather than guess. The diagnosis-label vs grounding-only flag drives purpose: grounding candidates generalize \texttt  {locate\_tooth}, diagnosis candidates would feed trace generation.}}{6}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:datasets}{{2}{6}{The data layer. Two sources are in use --- DENTEX (the only panoramic dataset pairing tooth position with all four reward-relevant pathology classes) and the Tufts Dental Database (per-tooth boxes plus an expert pathology layer whose periapical class maps onto the DENTEX taxonomy). Eight further public panoramic datasets are catalogued as candidates and deliberately \emph {not} used: each would be promoted only after its annotation semantics are verified end-to-end, and two loaders currently stop at exactly one open question rather than guess. The diagnosis-label vs grounding-only flag drives purpose: grounding candidates generalize \texttt {locate\_tooth}, diagnosis candidates would feed trace generation}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:datasets@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][4][]6}{}{}{}}
